Ethics notes

    - the phenomenon of the cosmic safehold evidences a source of morals
        : see @ ../text.brec
        ∵ as I work at explicating the artificial explanantia, I happen upon larger considerations
          that speak in their favour, giving me reason to believe that the safehold is indeed an artifact
          of creative design, considerations in which a feature of the safehold serves as evidence
          of that design and therewith of a source of morals in a universal or cosmic creator
            : re `(as I work).+(I happen.+on.+considerations.+in).+(favour)(, giving me reason.+)`s
              see `${1}.+${2}.+${3}.+${4}`sp @ ../text.brec
            ∵ those considerations reveal in the underlying theory of the artificial explanantia
              what is not immediately obvious, namely the following
                coherence
                    ∵ they reveal a [surprising] larger coincidence [that is] not immediately obvious
                        - among:
                            i) [practical] reason
                            ii) {[our] nature} | {natural selection}
                            iii) [cosmic] safehold
                        - in that:
                            - all relate [us] to the same end|object
                            - each does so optimally
                        - through optimal:
                            i) aiming|guiding [of us] toward it
                            ii) urging|forcing [of us] toward it
                                ?+ maybe [just] that practical logic inheres in it?
                                    : note : from notepad entry 2026-9-8d
                                    / that might suffice to explain (ii), leaving the design explanantia
                                      to focus on (i) and (iii) which they alone can explain
                                    ?+ maybe Taylor’s and Wright’s insights indicate this?
                            iii) facilitation of [[our] progress to] it
                    ∵ this [surprising] [larger] [triple] coincidence is accounted for [elegantly]
                      by each design explanans and thereby given [a] [theoretic] coherence
                    ∵ accounting for and giving [theoretic] coherence to a [surprising]
                      [larger] coincidence speaks in favour of the truth of an explanans
                symmetry
                    : see @ text.notes_boneyard.brec : speaks not in favour, after all
            ∵ revelation of this coherence (and explanatory power) in the other explanantia is unlikely
                ∵ they could hardly (in their explication) refer to practical reason as a causal factor
                    ∵ they are natural as opposed to artificial explanantia
    Anscombe’s challenge: the opportunity (presented by the safehold) to meet her challenge
      of a modern (re)grounding for moral law
        : re `Anscombe’s challenge` see doi:10.1017/9781139025065 : Darwall, Stephen.  2023.
          *Modern moral philosophy from Grotius to Kant.*  pp. 1-4
        : re `Anscombe’s challenge` see doi:10.1017/9781009543835 : Darwall, Stephen.  2025.
          *Modern moral philosophy in the nineteenth century.*  pp. 1-3
        - to read: \ in addition to Anscombe and Darwall
            \ 1958.  Anscombe, G.E.M.  ‘Modern moral philosophy.’
            + 1958.  Remnant, Peter.  ‘God and the moral law.’
                : see https://biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/cjt/04-1_023.pdf
            + 2011.  Murphy, Mark.  *God and moral law: on the theistic explanation of morality.*
                : see doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199693665.001.0001
            + 2017.  Rosen, Gideon.  *What is a moral law?.*
                : see doi:10.1093/oso/9780198805076.003.0006
            + 2022.  Driver, Julia.  *Moral theory.*
                : see https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-theory/
            \ 2023.  Darwall, Stephen.  *Modern moral philosophy from Grotius to Kant.*
            \ 2025.  Darwall, Stephen.  *Modern moral philosophy in the nineteenth century.*
            + re "Anscombe" "Modern moral philosophy"
    moral law, conditions
        a) a principle defining the criteria of right action
            : re `principle defining the criteria of right action`
              see `^*: re .moral law.+${same}`s @ ../text.brec
            : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @ ~/work/ethics/default/._/text.notes.brec
        •) its being absolute
            : re `absolute` see `^*: re .moral law.+a.+${same} principle`s @ ../text.brec
            , sub-conditions that suffice
                    : ad `suffice` : this set of sub-conditions of absoluteness is sufficient,
                      but might or might not be necessary, depending on whether ∃ a different set
                      that also is sufficient
                    b) its command (addressed demand) by our creator
                        : cf. `^*: re .moral law.+a divine ordinance`s @ ../text.brec
                        : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @
                          ~/work/ethics/normative/._/text.notes.brec
                    c) the creator’s backing of it by force
                        : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @
                          ~/work/ethics/applied/._/text.notes.brec
                - these suffice to remove any qualification of the principle and thereby make it absolute
                    / here the particular principle that meets condition (a) is a principle of logic
                      carrying the implicit qualification, ‘if one wishes to act logically’
                    ∵ together they make the principle obligatory without qualification
                        / here they make the principle of logic apply regardless of one’s wishes,
                          whether one likes it or not
                    admit+ however a qualification of scope (cosmic) re the case of mundane creators
                        | a scope ‘qualification’ of ‘cosmic’ needs no admission
                            ∵ already it inheres (implicitly) in our concept of moral law
                                ∵ it attaches also in|to the case of God’s moral law,
                                  the scope of which is not truly absolute
                                    ∵ that law is scoped to God’s creation (or its morally competent
                                      creatures) as opposed to all that exists *including God*
                                        ∵ we do not think of that law applying to God
                                          as it would apply to us
                            + ∴ explicate the principle’s absolute scope (in keeping with the concept)
                              as being all of creation, or all of creation’s morally competent creatures,
                              or whatever the correct term
                        | in that case the qualification must be elevated to the moral law,
                          making it cosmic as opposed to universal in scope
                            ∵ only a universal creator (God) could obligate universally
                            / though the difference between cosmic and universal could hardly matter
                              in practice
                        / yet (a mitigation) the scope of obligation could at least be multi-cosmic,
                          in being lineally inherited
    structure of the ethic
        - division of concept into conditions → division of ethics into fields
            : re `division of concept into (conditions)` see `^^moral law, ${same}$`i
            - consideration of the latter *follow* on that of the former
                / rather than consider first the customary division of ethics,
                  then, in that light, the division of the concept
                - the customary fields of ethics I reuse if (and as) applicable
                    : ad `\(and as\)` : I wear the shoe as it fits
            - two of the conditions of moral law (that analysis yields) apportion cleanly
              between two fields of ethics (normative and applied)
                - the command condition is proper to normative ethics
                    ∴ the normative ethic is the proper place for seeking the creator’s reason
                        : re `creator’s reason` see `^*• God had reason.+to make the safehold$`s @
                          ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
                        : re `creator’s reason` see `^*• cosmocrafters have reason.+to.+cosmocraft`s @
                          ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
                - the enforcement condition is proper to applied ethics
                    ∵ outside the norms that guide, the only question is the facts
                      that move us to conform
